{#
    `.. contents::` — what is on this page.

    A `<nav>` and not a `<div>`: it is navigation, and it is the second lot on
    the page, so it says which one it is. The class the core writes stays on
    it — `document.css` maps what a renderer emits, and renaming it would only
    move the same rule to a name no other tool knows.

    `sds-aside` is the column it stands in where the page has the room for one.
    It spans the document, so the list can rest anywhere in it; the nav is what
    rests, and is a second element for that reason. Narrower, the box is not
    there at all and the list is a block where the author wrote it.
#}
{%- set label = node.caption ? renderNode(node.caption)|striptags|trim : 'On this page' -%}
<div class="sds-aside">
    <nav class="contents" aria-label="{{ label }}">
        {#- The list says what it is a list of. It is a label over links rather
            than a heading over prose, which is the register a rail names its
            own pages in — and on a page with sixty headings the reader has to
            know which of the two lists they are looking at. -#}
        <p class="sds-label">{{ label }}</p>
        {%- import "body/menu/toc-entries.html.twig" as toc -%}
        {{ toc.entries(node.menuEntries, 'sections') }}
    </nav>
</div>
